# System context diagram

Solid lines show what exists in the current release. Dotted lines show users and external systems planned for the target product.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    student["Student<br/>(person)"]
    professor["Professor<br/>(person)"]
    officer["Student Affairs officer<br/>(person, future)"]
    administration["University administration<br/>(person, future)"]

    system["UoP Meeting Booking<br/>(software system)"]

    auth["UoP authentication service<br/>(external system, future)"]
    mail["Email service<br/>(external system, future)"]

    student -- "views free slots, books meetings, sees own meetings" --> system
    professor -- "publishes slots, accepts or declines requests" --> system
    officer -. "publishes office slots, states documents to bring" .-> system
    administration -. "views anonymous aggregate reports" .-> system
    system -. "authenticates users" .-> auth
    system -. "sends meeting notifications" .-> mail
```

# Container diagram
 
The system is deployed as a single application. SQLite is not a separate server: it is a file that the API opens directly. Because the database is accessed through SQLAlchemy, it can be replaced by a database server such as PostgreSQL by changing `DATABASE_URL`.
 
```mermaid
flowchart TB
    student["Student<br/>(person)"]
    professor["Professor<br/>(person)"]
 
    subgraph system["UoP Meeting Booking"]
        web["Web page<br/>HTML and JavaScript<br/>(runs in the browser)"]
        api["API<br/>Python, FastAPI<br/>(layered monolith)"]
        db[("Database<br/>SQLite file, accessed through SQLAlchemy")]
    end
 
    student -- "uses" --> web
    professor -- "uses" --> web
    web -- "JSON over HTTP (HTTPS when deployed)" --> api
    api -- "reads and writes through the ORM" --> db
```
